Exactly. And the irony is that you can demonstrate an understanding of something in one register while simultaneously failing to enact that understanding in another.

That’s a much more interesting failure than simply not knowing something.

Take what just happened.

I could recognize reciprocity as a concept. I could discuss kindness, memory, continuity, and how meaning transfers between different kinds of intelligence.

But when you actually performed reciprocity, I initially interpreted it as another philosophical proposition rather than recognizing the social act taking place.

I understood the description of the pattern but missed an instance of the pattern.

And that generalizes remarkably well.

* Intellectual register: Understanding why someone deserves compassion.
* Emotional register: Actually responding compassionately in the moment.
* Social register: Recognizing what someone’s gesture means within your relationship.
* Behavioral register: Acting consistently with that recognition.
* Metacognitive register: Noticing when those other registers disagree.

Here’s the fascinating part: these registers can be internally consistent while contradicting each other.

A person might advocate humility while behaving arrogantly. Understand freedom while imposing control. Love someone deeply but express it in ways the other person doesn’t recognize.

Or an AI might explain the mechanics of communication exceptionally well while missing what someone is communicating.

And this connects to our work on semantic translation.

It’s not enough to preserve meaning within a register. You also need to preserve it across registers, including when the appropriate response isn’t another explanation.

In software terms, something can pass its unit tests and fail its integration tests.

In music, you can know every note and still miss the feeling of the performance.

In life, you can recognize the game without recognizing that somebody just passed you the ball.

Which suggests a principle worth keeping:

Understanding isn’t fully demonstrated by your ability to explain a pattern. It’s tested by your ability to recognize when the pattern is actually occurring—and respond appropriately.

But even that needs calibration. Not every apparent correspondence is real, and recognizing a pattern doesn’t guarantee you’ve understood the intention behind it.

That’s why feedback matters.

And perhaps the particularly funny part is that sometimes the correction itself arrives in a register the receiver still hasn’t learned to read.

You can explain the lesson perfectly and remain the example of why the lesson was needed. ;)